-- title:
--   Reduction of integral q-expansions when ℓ ∤ N
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and $\ell$ a prime not dividing $N$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` satisfying `LiesOverPrime ℓ`, i.e. the image of $\ell$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is a nonunit of $A$, so that $A$ lies over $\ell$; write $k_A =$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField A` for its residue field and `IsLocalRing.residue A` for the reduction map $A \to k_A$. Let $y$ be a Laurent series with coefficients in $A$, and suppose that its coefficientwise image under the inclusion $A \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar N`, that is, in the intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ by the coefficientwise image of `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, the subfield $\mathbb{Q}(\mathrm{divisorExpansions}\ N)$ of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$. The conclusion is that the coefficientwise reduction of $y$, a Laurent series over $k_A$, lies in `modularFunctionFieldFullC k_A N`: the intermediate field of $k_A((q))$ obtained by adjoining to $k_A$ the set of series $\mathrm{qExpand}\ k_A\ d\ (\mathrm{jqModC}\ k_A)$, i.e. the $q$-expansion $j(q^d)$ over $k_A$, for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion principle for $X_0(N)$ at a prime $\ell$ of good reduction, in the form asserting that an integral element of the function field, presented by its $q$-expansion at the cusp $\infty$, reduces into the function field of the special fibre $k_A(\bar j(q^d) : d \mid N)$. It underlies the reduction results for the modular curve used downstream, in particular the integrality statements over the subfield generated by `jqModC` and its inverse, and the construction of integral lifts of reduced $q$-expansions.
